<com:TContent ID="body" >

<h1>Collections</h1>
<p class="block-content">
Collection is a basic data structure in programming. In traditional PHP programming, array is used widely to represent collection data structure. A PHP array is a mix of cardinal-indexed array and hash table.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
To enable object-oriented manipulation of collections, PRADO provides a set of powerful collection classes. Among them, the <tt>TList</tt> and <tt>TMap</tt> are the most fundamental and usually serve as the base classes for other collection classes. Since many PRADO components have properties that are of collection type, it is very important for developers to master the usage of PRADO collection classes.
</p>

<h2>Using <tt>TList</tt></h2>
<p class="block-content">
A <tt>TList</tt> object represents a cardinal-indexed array, i.e., an array (object) with the index 0, 1, 2, ...
</p>
<p class="block-content">
<tt>TList</tt> may be used like a PHP array. For example,
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content" id="code1">
$list=new TList; // create a list object
...
$item=$list[$index];  // read the item at the specified index
$list[]=$item;  // append the item at the end
$list[$index]=$item; // replace the item at the specified index
unset($list[$index]); // remove the item at $index
if(isset($list[$index])) // test if the list has an item at $index
foreach($list as $index=>$item) // traverse each item in the list
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
To obtain the number of items in the list, use the <tt>Count</tt> property. Note, do not use <tt>count($list)</tt>, as it always returns 1.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
In addition, <tt>TList</tt> implements a few commonly used convenient methods for manipulating the data in a list. These include
</p>
<ul id="u1" class="block-content">
  <li><tt>clear()</tt>: removes all items in the list.</li>
  <li><tt>contains()</tt>: tests if the list contains the specified item.</li>
  <li><tt>indexOf()</tt>: obtains the zero-based index of the specified item in the list.</li>
  <li><tt>toArray()</tt>: returns an array representation of the items in the list.</li>
  <li><tt>copyFrom()</tt>: populates the list with data from an array or traversable object (including <tt>TList</tt>). Existing items will be removed first.</li>
  <li><tt>mergeWith()</tt>: appends the list with data from an array or traversable object (including <tt>TList</tt>).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Using <tt>TList</tt>-based component properties</h3>
<p class="block-content">
As aforementioned, many PRADO component properties are based on <tt>TList</tt> or <tt>TList</tt>-derived collection classes. These properties all share the above usages.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
For example, <tt>TControl</tt> (the base class for all PRADO controls) has a property called <tt>Controls</tt> which represents the collection of child controls. The type of <tt>Controls</tt> is <tt>TControlCollection</tt> which extends <tt>TList</tt>. Therefore, to append a new child control, we can use the following,
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content" id="code2">
$control->Controls[]=$newControl;
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
To traverse through the child controls, we can use,
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content" id="code3">
foreach($control->Controls as $childControl) ...
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
Another example is the <tt>Items</tt> property, available in list controls, <tt>TRepeater</tt>, <tt>TDataList</tt> and <tt>TDataGrid</tt>. In these controls, the ancestor class of <tt>Items</tt> is <tt>TList</tt>.
</p>

<h3>Extending <tt>TList</tt></h3>
<p class="block-content">
Often, we want to extend <tt>TList</tt> to perform additional operations for each addition or removal of an item. The only methods that the child class needs to override are <tt>insertAt()</tt> and <tt>removeAt()</tt>. For example, to ensure the list only contains items that are of <tt>TControl</tt> type, we can override <tt>insertAt()</tt> as follows,
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content" id="code4">
public function insertAt($index,$item)
{
    if($item instanceof TControl)
        parent::insertAt($index,$item)
    else
        throw new Exception('TControl required.');
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>


<h2>Using <tt>TMap</tt></h2>
<p class="block-content">
A <tt>TMap</tt> object represents a hash table (or we say string-indexed array).
</p>
<p class="block-content">
Similar to <tt>TList</tt>, <tt>TMap</tt> may be used like an array,
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content" id="code5">
$map=new TMap; // create a map object
...
$map[$key]=$value; // add a key-value pair
unset($map[$key]); // remove the value with the specified key
if(isset($map[$key])) // if the map contains the key
foreach($map as $key=>$value) // traverse the items in the map
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
The <tt>Count</tt> property gives the number of items in the map while the <tt>Keys</tt> property returns a list of keys used in the map.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
The following methods are provided by <tt>TMap</tt> for convenience,
</p>
<ul id="u2" class="block-content">
  <li><tt>clear()</tt>: removes all items in the map.</li>
  <li><tt>contains()</tt>: tests if the map contains the specified key.</li>
  <li><tt>toArray()</tt>: returns an array representation of the items in the map.</li>
  <li><tt>copyFrom()</tt>: populates the map with data from an array or traversable object (including <tt>TMap</tt>). Existing items will be removed first.</li>
  <li><tt>mergeWith()</tt>: appends the map with data from an array or traversable object (including <tt>TMap</tt>).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Using of <tt>TAttributeCollection</tt></h3>
<p class="block-content">
<tt>TAttributeCollection</tt> is a special class extending from <tt>TMap</tt>. It is mainly used by the <tt>Attributes</tt> property of <tt>TControl</tt>.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
Besides the normal functionalities provided by <tt>TMap</tt>, <tt>TAttributeCollection</tt> allows you to get and set collection items like getting and setting properties. For example,
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content" id="code6">
$collection->Label='value'; // equivalent to: $collection['Label']='value';
echo $collection->Label; // equivalent to: echo $collection['Label'];
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
Note, in the above <tt>$collection</tt> does NOT have a <tt>Label</tt> property.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
Unlike <tt>TMap</tt>, keys in <tt>TAttributeCollection</tt> are case-insensitive. Therefore, <tt>$collection->Label</tt> is equivalent to <tt>$collection->LABEL</tt>.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
Because of the above new features, when dealing with the <tt>Attributes</tt> property of controls, we may take advantage of the subproperty concept and configure control attribute values in a template as follows,
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content" id="code7">
&lt;com:TButton Attributes.onclick="if(!confirm('Are you sure?')) return false;" .../&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
which adds an attribute named <tt>onclick</tt> to the <tt>TButton</tt> control.
</p>

<h2>Weak collections</h2>
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.3.0" />
<p class="block-content">
A weak collection holds an object as a weak reference, so the collection does not keep the
object from being garbage-collected. A list or map holds a strong reference to its items,
so an item stays alive while the collection holds it. A weak collection is useful for a
registry of objects that other code owns, such as event handlers, where the collection
follows the objects instead of keeping them alive.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
<tt>TWeakList</tt> is an integer-indexed list that stores its objects as weak references.
When an object is collected, the list either removes the entry or replaces it with
<tt>null</tt>, which the constructor mode selects. The default mode removes the entry, so
the list holds only live objects. A list item that is not an object is stored directly.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
use Prado\Collections\TWeakList;

$list = new TWeakList();
$list->add($object);   // stored as a weak reference
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.3.3" />
<p class="block-content">
<tt>TWeakMap</tt> is a key-value map that stores its object values as weak references. The
keys are held directly. A non-object value, and a <tt>Closure</tt>, are stored directly.
The <tt>DiscardInvalid</tt> property sets what happens when a value is collected. When it
is true, the entry is removed. When it is false, the entry stays and the value reads as
<tt>null</tt>.
</p>

<h2>The null object</h2>
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.3.3" />
<p class="block-content">
<tt>TNull</tt> is an object that stands for nothing. It fills a place in a container that
does not allow <tt>null</tt>, so the container holds a value that behaves like
<tt>null</tt>. <tt>TNull</tt> is a singleton, so one instance exists for the process. The
<tt>TNull::null()</tt> and <tt>TNull::singleton()</tt> methods return the instance.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
use Prado\Collections\TNull;

$nothing = TNull::null();
TNull::is_null($nothing);   // true for a TNull instance or PHP null
TNull::empty($nothing);     // true for TNull, null, false, 0, '', or []
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>Priority collections</h2>
<com:SinceVersion Version="3.2" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TPriorityList</tt> and <tt>TPriorityMap</tt> keep their items in priority order rather
than insertion order. A priority is a number, and a lower number comes first, so -10
precedes 0 precedes 10. The default priority is 10, and items added without one keep their
insertion order among themselves. A priority list used without priorities behaves exactly
like a <tt>TList</tt>.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
This is how the framework orders things that several parties contribute to.
<a href="?page=Fundamentals.Behaviors">Behaviors</a> attach their event handlers at the
behavior's priority, and an <a href="?page=Advanced.Auth">authorization rule</a> uses a
priority so that a deny-everything rule stays last however many rules are added after it.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
use Prado\Collections\TPriorityList;

$list = new TPriorityList();
$list-&gt;add('middle');           // the default priority, 10
$list-&gt;add('first', 5);
$list-&gt;add('last', 20);

foreach ($list as $item) {
    echo $item;                 // first, middle, last
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Method</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
<tr><td><tt>add($item, $priority)</tt></td><td>Adds an item at a priority, or at the default when none is given.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>priorityOf($item)</tt></td><td>The priority an item was added at.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>priorityAt($index)</tt></td><td>The priority of the item at a flattened index.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>itemsAtPriority($priority)</tt></td><td>Just the items at one priority.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>insertBefore($target, $item)</tt>, <tt>insertAfter($target, $item)</tt></td><td>Places an item next to another one, taking that item's priority.</td></tr>
</table>

<p class="block-content">
Priorities are rounded to a precision, eight decimal places by default, so a priority can
be adjusted finely without colliding with a neighbour. Reading the collection as a plain
array flattens it in priority order and caches the result, which is why iteration is no
more costly than over an ordinary list.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TPriorityMap</tt> applies the same ordering to keyed items. A class that wants its
instances placed automatically implements <tt>IPriorityItem</tt>, and the collection reads
the priority from the item instead of from the call.
</p>

<h2>Stacks and queues</h2>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TStack</tt> is last in, first out. <tt>TQueue</tt> is first in, first out. Both wrap a
plain array, count with <tt>getCount</tt>, test membership with <tt>contains</tt>, read the
next item without removing it with <tt>peek</tt>, empty themselves with <tt>clear</tt>, and
can be iterated and converted with <tt>toArray</tt>.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th><tt>TStack</tt></th><th><tt>TQueue</tt></th></tr>
<tr><td><tt>push($item)</tt></td><td><tt>enqueue($item)</tt></td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>pop()</tt></td><td><tt>dequeue()</tt></td></tr>
</table>

<p class="block-content">
Taking from an empty one raises a <tt>TInvalidOperationException</tt>, so check
<tt>getCount</tt> first.
</p>

<h2>Paged lists</h2>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TPagedList</tt> presents one page of a larger list. <tt>PageSize</tt> sets how many
items a page holds and <tt>CurrentPageIndex</tt> selects the page, with <tt>gotoPage</tt>,
<tt>nextPage</tt> and <tt>previousPage</tt> to move between them. <tt>PageCount</tt>,
<tt>IsFirstPage</tt> and <tt>IsLastPage</tt> describe where the list currently is.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
With <tt>CustomPaging</tt> set, the list no longer holds every item. It raises
<tt>OnFetchData</tt> when it needs a page, the handler supplies just that page, and
<tt>VirtualCount</tt> tells the list how many items exist in total. <tt>OnPageIndexChanged</tt>
reports a move. This is the same division of labour as the custom paging described under
<a href="?page=Controls.Data">Data Controls</a>, at the collection level rather than the
control level.
</p>

<h2>Subscriptions</h2>
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.3.0" />
<p class="block-content">
A subscription adds something to a target and removes it later. The <tt>subscribe</tt>
method adds it, and the <tt>unsubscribe</tt> method removes it. A subscription also
unsubscribes when it is dereferenced, so what it added is removed when the subscription
goes out of scope. This ties the lifetime of an addition to the lifetime of an object. The
three subscription classes add to an array, to a collection, and to an event.
</p>

<h3><tt>TArraySubscription</tt></h3>
<p class="block-content">
<tt>TArraySubscription</tt> adds an item to an array. It works on a PHP array by reference,
an <tt>ArrayAccess</tt> object, a <tt>TList</tt>, or a <tt>TMap</tt>. When the array is
associative, the subscription saves the original value and restores it on unsubscribe. A
subscription to a priority collection keeps the item's priority, and restores the original
priority on unsubscribe.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
use Prado\Collections\TArraySubscription;
use Prado\Collections\TPriorityMap;

$map = new TPriorityMap(['key' => 'original value']);
$subscription = new TArraySubscription($map, 'key', 'new value', priority: 5);
// $map['key'] is now 'new value'
$subscription->unsubscribe();
// $map['key'] is 'original value' again
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h3><tt>TCollectionSubscription</tt></h3>
<p class="block-content">
<tt>TCollectionSubscription</tt> extends <tt>TArraySubscription</tt> and does the same
thing without passing the target by reference. It works on an <tt>ArrayAccess</tt> object,
such as a <tt>TList</tt> or a <tt>TMap</tt>, and it does not work on a PHP array. Its
<tt>Collection</tt> property reads the target without a reference. Use it when the target
is an object rather than a PHP array.
</p>

<h3><tt>TEventSubscription</tt></h3>
<p class="block-content">
<tt>TEventSubscription</tt> extends <tt>TCollectionSubscription</tt> and adds an event
handler to a component's event. The handler stays attached while the subscription exists,
and it detaches when the subscription unsubscribes or is dereferenced. This attaches a
handler for a limited time, such as within a loop or a single operation.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
use Prado\TEventSubscription;

$subscription = new TEventSubscription($component, 'onChange', function ($sender, $param) {
    // runs while the subscription is alive
});
// ...
$subscription->unsubscribe();   // the handler is detached
</com:TTextHighlighter>

</com:TContent>
